1750	  /* ── tone chip ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1751	     The role/status pill as the concept screens actually draw it, which is
1752	     NOT what `.badge` above does. Measured off the owner's Team concept: an
1753	     "Admin" pill is a very dark tinted fill (rgb 17,15,64) with a 1px border
1754	     a step brighter (rgb 34,28,97), a FULL-saturation icon (rgb 145,42,247)
1755	     and LIGHT text in the same hue (rgb 190,120,232). `.badge` inverts that —
1756	     a saturated fill with dark text — which is what reads as a loud sticker
1757	     next to the concept's restrained chip.
1758	
1759	     One shape, four tones, driven by --chip-h/--chip-s so a tone is three
1760	     numbers rather than three hand-mixed hexes. The icon inside takes
1761	     `currentColor`; set it apart with .chip-tone-mark. */
1762	  /* Values are the concept's, measured: an Admin chip's fill is rgb(17,15,64)
1763	     = hsl(242 62% 15%), its border rgb(34,28,97) = hsl(245 55% 24%), its text
1764	     rgb(190,120,232) = hsl(277 71% 69%). The fill/border sit a touch cooler
1765	     than the mark and label, which is why --chip-hm exists: tones that need
1766	     it push the mark and text a few degrees off the fill's hue rather than
1767	     rendering the whole chip as one flat colour. */
1768	  .chip-tone {
1769	    --chip-h: 218; --chip-s: 90%; --chip-hm: var(--chip-h);
1770	    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 7px;
1771	    height: 26px; padding: 0 11px; border-radius: 8px;
1772	    font-size: 11.5px; font-weight: 650; white-space: nowrap;
1773	    color: hsl(var(--chip-hm) calc(var(--chip-s) * 0.78) 72%);
